    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>Homocysteine is a sulfur-containing non-proteinogenic &alpha;-amino acid formed exclusively during the intracellular catabolism of methionine. In the methionine cycle, L-methionine condenses with ATP via methionine adenosyltransferase (MAT) to generate <em>S</em>-adenosylmethionine (SAM), the primary biological methyl donor. Methyltransferase reactions transfer the active methyl group from SAM to diverse cellular acceptors, forming <em>S</em>-adenosylhomocysteine (SAH). SAH hydrolase then reversibly cleaves SAH into adenosine and free homocysteine.</p>\n<p>Intracellular homocysteine sits at a pivotal metabolic branch point with two competing pathways: remethylation to methionine or irreversible transsulfuration to cysteine. In remethylation, homocysteine is converted back to methionine by methionine synthase (MTR), which utilizes <em>N</em><sup>5</sup>-methyltetrahydrofolate (<em>N</em><sup>5</sup>-methyl-THF) as a methyl donor and requires methylcobalamin (vitamin B<sub>12</sub>) as an essential cofactor. In the liver, betaine-homocysteine <em>S</em>-methyltransferase (BHMT) provides an alternative folate-independent remethylation pathway utilizing betaine (trimethylglycine). In the transsulfuration pathway, cystathionine &beta;-synthase (CBS) condenses homocysteine with L-serine to form cystathionine. CBS is a unique homotetrameric hemoprotein that requires pyridoxal 5&prime;-phosphate (PLP, vitamin B<sub>6</sub>) as a catalytic cofactor and is allosterically activated by SAM. Cystathionine is subsequently cleaved by cystathionine &gamma;-lyase (cystathionase, also PLP-dependent) into L-cysteine, &alpha;-ketobutyrate, and ammonia.</p>\n<p>Classic homocystinuria is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the <em>CBS</em> gene. Defective CBS activity leads to massive accumulation of homocysteine, homocystine (the oxidized disulfide dimer), and upstream methionine, while cysteine becomes a conditionally essential amino acid. Major clinical features include marfanoid habitus, ectopia lentis (inferomedial subluxation of the ocular lens), intellectual disability, and life-threatening arterial and venous thromboembolism. Thromboembolism is driven by endothelial injury, reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation, and homocysteine thiolactone-mediated homocysteinylation of extracellular matrix proteins. Approximately 50% of patients respond biochemically to pharmacologic pyridoxine (vitamin B<sub>6</sub>) therapy.</p>\n<p>Investigators evaluated cultured human vascular endothelial cells and liver biopsy homogenates from healthy controls, B<sub>6</sub>-responsive homocystinuric patients (<em>CBS</em>-I278T), and B<sub>6</sub>-non-responsive patients (<em>CBS</em>-T191M). Cells were treated with basal medium, pyridoxine (50 &mu;M), or betaine (2 mM). Enzymatic activity, amino acid concentrations, endothelial ROS generation, and thrombin peak generation were measured (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Biochemical, enzymatic, and prothrombotic parameters across CBS genotypes and therapeutic regimens</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Genotype / Patient Cohort</th><th>In Vitro Treatment</th><th>CBS Catalytic Activity (% Control)</th><th>Total Plasma Homocysteine (&mu;M)</th><th>Plasma Methionine (&mu;M)</th><th>Endothelial ROS Generation (% Control)</th><th>Thrombin Peak Generation (nM)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Control (Wild-Type)</td><td>None</td><td>100.0 &plusmn; 4.5%</td><td>8.5 &plusmn; 0.8</td><td>24.2 &plusmn; 1.8</td><td>100 &plusmn; 5%</td><td>145 &plusmn; 12</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Control (Wild-Type)</td><td>Pyridoxine (50 &mu;M)</td><td>102.1 &plusmn; 4.8%</td><td>8.2 &plusmn; 0.7</td><td>23.8 &plusmn; 1.6</td><td>98 &plusmn; 4%</td><td>142 &plusmn; 10</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Responsive (<em>CBS</em>-I278T)</td><td>None</td><td>3.8 &plusmn; 0.5%</td><td>148.5 &plusmn; 11.2</td><td>185.4 &plusmn; 14.2</td><td>285 &plusmn; 16%</td><td>385 &plusmn; 24</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Responsive (<em>CBS</em>-I278T)</td><td>Pyridoxine (50 &mu;M)</td><td>32.4 &plusmn; 2.6%</td><td>28.4 &plusmn; 2.5</td><td>42.1 &plusmn; 3.5</td><td>128 &plusmn; 8%</td><td>178 &plusmn; 14</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Responsive (<em>CBS</em>-I278T)</td><td>Betaine (2 mM)</td><td>4.1 &plusmn; 0.6%</td><td>45.2 &plusmn; 3.8</td><td>295.6 &plusmn; 21.0</td><td>152 &plusmn; 11%</td><td>210 &plusmn; 16</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Non-responsive (<em>CBS</em>-T191M)</td><td>None</td><td>< 0.5%</td><td>192.4 &plusmn; 15.0</td><td>210.8 &plusmn; 16.5</td><td>340 &plusmn; 22%</td><td>440 &plusmn; 28</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Non-responsive (<em>CBS</em>-T191M)</td><td>Pyridoxine (50 &mu;M)</td><td>< 0.5%</td><td>188.1 &plusmn; 14.6</td><td>205.2 &plusmn; 15.8</td><td>332 &plusmn; 20%</td><td>425 &plusmn; 26</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Non-responsive (<em>CBS</em>-T191M)</td><td>Betaine (2 mM)</td><td>< 0.5%</td><td>58.6 &plusmn; 4.9</td><td>360.4 &plusmn; 26.2</td><td>168 &plusmn; 12%</td><td>235 &plusmn; 18</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
