    "topic": "Purine De Novo Synthesis: PRPP Synthetase & Glutamine-PRPP Amidotransferase Feedback Control",
    "sheet": 4,
    "label": "Biological and Biochemical Foundations of Living Systems",
    "examId": "bb60_7",
    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 4 (Questions 19&ndash;24)</div>\n<p>De novo purine nucleotide biosynthesis synthesizes the bicyclic purine ring (adenine and guanine) directly upon an activated ribose scaffold. The pathway initiates with the conversion of &alpha;-D-ribose 5-phosphate, generated by the pentose phosphate pathway, to 5-phospho-&alpha;-D-ribosyl 1-pyrophosphate (PRPP) by PRPP synthetase (ribose-phosphate pyrophosphokinase). This reaction transfers a pyrophosphate moiety from ATP to carbon-1 of ribose 5-phosphate, releasing AMP. PRPP synthetase is allosterically activated by inorganic phosphate (P<sub>i</sub>) and subject to feedback inhibition by purine ribonucleotides (ADP, GDP).</p>\n<p>The committed, rate-limiting step of purine biosynthesis is catalyzed by glutamine-PRPP amidotransferase (GPAT). GPAT transfers an amino group from the amide side chain of L-glutamine to PRPP, displacing pyrophosphate with inversion of configuration at C-1 to form 5-phospho-&beta;-D-ribosylamine (PRA), L-glutamate, and inorganic pyrophosphate (PP<sub>i</sub>). Rapid hydrolysis of PP<sub>i</sub> by ubiquitous inorganic pyrophosphatase pulls the equilibrium forward. GPAT is allosterically activated by its substrate PRPP and synergistically feedback-inhibited by the end products AMP, ADP, GMP, and GDP. Structural studies demonstrate that GPAT contains two distinct allosteric inhibition pockets: the 'A-site' (binding adenine nucleotides) and the 'G-site' (binding guanine nucleotides). Simultaneous occupancy of both sites drives the assembly of active monomers into an inactive dimer, exerting potent synergistic inhibition.</p>\n<p>Across nine subsequent enzymatic steps, the purine ring is assembled using glycine (contributing C4, C5, and N7), <em>N</em><sup>10</sup>-formyl-THF (contributing C2 and C8), glutamine (contributing N3 and N9), aspartate (contributing N1), and bicarbonate/CO<sub>2</sub> (contributing C6), yielding inosine monophosphate (IMP). IMP is channeled into either AMP or GMP via cross-regulated pathways: GTP fuels adenylosuccinate synthetase in AMP synthesis, whereas ATP powers GMP synthetase in GMP synthesis. Gain-of-function superactivity mutations in PRPP synthetase 1 (<em>PRPS1</em>) abolish nucleotide feedback inhibition, causing massive purine overproduction and juvenile gout.</p>\n<p>Biochemists investigated recombinant human GPAT and PRPS1 (wild-type vs superactive N114S mutant). Steady-state enzyme kinetics, nucleotide inhibition constants (<em>IC</em><sub>50</sub>), and intracellular PRPP accumulation in patient skin fibroblasts were quantified (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Kinetic constants, synergistic feedback inhibition, and PRPP accumulation in GPAT and PRPS1 variants</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Enzyme / Experimental Condition</th><th><em>K</em><sub>m</sub> for Substrate (&mu;M)</th><th>Single Inhibitor <em>IC</em><sub>50</sub> (AMP, &mu;M)</th><th>Single Inhibitor <em>IC</em><sub>50</sub> (GMP, &mu;M)</th><th>Synergistic <em>IC</em><sub>50</sub> (AMP + GMP, &mu;M)</th><th>Fibroblast [PRPP] (&mu;M)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>GPAT (Basal WT)</td><td>185 &plusmn; 12 (PRPP)</td><td>240 &plusmn; 15</td><td>210 &plusmn; 14</td><td>18.5 &plusmn; 1.2</td><td>5.2 &plusmn; 0.4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>GPAT + 5 mM P<sub>i</sub></td><td>95 &plusmn; 8 (PRPP)</td><td>480 &plusmn; 28</td><td>420 &plusmn; 25</td><td>42.0 &plusmn; 2.8</td><td>8.4 &plusmn; 0.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>GPAT (Dimeric Inactive Mutant)</td><td>> 1,500 (PRPP)</td><td>35 &plusmn; 3</td><td>28 &plusmn; 2</td><td>4.2 &plusmn; 0.3</td><td>3.1 &plusmn; 0.2</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>PRPS1 (Wild-Type)</td><td>32 &plusmn; 2 (Ribose-5-P)</td><td>45 &plusmn; 4 (ADP)</td><td>62 &plusmn; 5 (GDP)</td><td>12.0 &plusmn; 0.9</td><td>5.2 &plusmn; 0.4</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>PRPS1 (Superactive N114S)</td><td>30 &plusmn; 2 (Ribose-5-P)</td><td>> 2,000 (ADP)</td><td>> 2,000 (GDP)</td><td>> 1,500</td><td>78.4 &plusmn; 5.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>PRPS1 (N114S) + 6-Mercaptopurine</td><td>31 &plusmn; 2 (Ribose-5-P)</td><td>140 &plusmn; 10 (TIMP)</td><td>125 &plusmn; 9 (TIMP)</td><td>22.4 &plusmn; 1.6</td><td>14.8 &plusmn; 1.1</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
