        "e": "<strong>Precursor origins of the purine ring (Topic 578):</strong> The purine bicyclic core (fused 6-membered pyrimidine and 5-membered imidazole rings) is constructed piece by piece from several metabolic donors: (1) Entire glycine molecule: contributes carbons C4, C5, and nitrogen N7; (2) $N^{10}$-formyl-tetrahydrofolate: contributes carbons C2 and C8; (3) L-Glutamine amide nitrogens: contribute nitrogens N3 and N9; (4) L-Aspartate: contributes nitrogen N1; and (5) Respiratory bicarbonate/$\\text{CO}_2$: contributes carbon C6. Knowing these specific donors is critical for understanding the mechanism of folate antimetabolite drugs (like methotrexate) and glutamine analogs (like azaserine).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A scrambles the contributions; glycine contributes C4-C5-N7 (not C2/C8), and formyl-THF donates C2/C8.<br>&bull; B misassigns aspartate to N3/N9 (which come from glutamine) and bicarbonate to N1 (which comes from aspartate; bicarbonate has no nitrogen).<br>&bull; C incorrectly attributes C4/C5 to glutamine and N1 to glycine."
