    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>Unlike purine biosynthesis, where the purine ring is assembled directly upon a ribose scaffold, de novo pyrimidine biosynthesis synthesizes the six-membered pyrimidine heterocycle (orotate) as a free base prior to attachment to PRPP. The pathway initiates in the cytoplasm with the formation of carbamoyl phosphate, catalyzed by carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II (CPS-II). CPS-II represents the committed, rate-limiting step of pyrimidine synthesis and resides on the cytosolic trifunctional enzyme CAD (containing Carbamoyl-phosphate synthetase II, Aspartate transcarbamylase, and Dihydroorotase domains).</p>\n<p>CPS-II utilizes L-glutamine, bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>), and two ATP molecules to generate carbamoyl phosphate, L-glutamate, two ADPs, and inorganic phosphate (P<sub>i</sub>). CPS-II is allosterically activated by PRPP and ATP, and feedback-inhibited by UTP. Cell cycle progression stimulates CAD through MAPK/ERK-mediated phosphorylation at Thr456, which relieves UTP inhibition, whereas PKA phosphorylation at Ser1406 enhances inhibition. In the second step, aspartate transcarbamylase (ATCase) condenses carbamoyl phosphate with L-aspartate to form carbamoyl aspartate. Dihydroorotase then catalyzes intramolecular cyclization to form dihydroorotate.</p>\n<p>Dihydroorotate is oxidized to orotate by dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (DHODH), a flavoprotein anchored to the inner mitochondrial membrane that donates electrons directly to ubiquinone (coenzyme Q) in the respiratory chain. Orotate returns to the cytosol, where UMP synthase&mdash;a bifunctional enzyme possessing orotate phosphoribosyltransferase (OPRT) and orotidine 5&prime;-monophosphate decarboxylase (ODCase) catalytic domains&mdash;couples orotate with PRPP to form orotidine 5&prime;-monophosphate (OMP), followed by irreversible decarboxylation to uridine monophosphate (UMP).</p>\n<p>Hereditary orotic aciduria is an autosomal recessive disorder caused by deficiency of UMP synthase. Accumulation of orotic acid produces severe megaloblastic anemia refractory to vitamin B<sub>12</sub> and folate, developmental delay, and orotic acid crystalluria. In contrast, ornithine transcarbamoylase (OTC) deficiency, an X-linked urea cycle defect, also causes orotic aciduria due to mitochondrial carbamoyl phosphate spillover into the cytosol, but is clinically distinguished by catastrophic hyperammonemia.</p>\n<p>Investigators evaluated wild-type and UMP synthase-deficient patient fibroblasts cultured with varying concentrations of uridine, cytidine, or the DHODH inhibitor teriflunomide. Pyrimidine intermediate pools, CAD catalytic activity, and [<sup>3</sup>H]thymidine incorporation into genomic DNA were measured (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Pyrimidine biosynthetic flux, intracellular nucleotide pools, and DNA synthesis across conditions</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Cell Line / Treatment Condition</th><th>CPS-II Specific Activity (nmol/min/mg)</th><th>Intracellular Orotate (&mu;M)</th><th>Intracellular UTP (&mu;M)</th><th>[<sup>3</sup>H]Thymidine Incorporation (% Control)</th><th>Cellular Morphology / Phenotype</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Wild-Type, Untreated</td><td>14.8 &plusmn; 1.1</td><td>1.2 &plusmn; 0.1</td><td>450 &plusmn; 28</td><td>100.0 &plusmn; 5.2%</td><td>Normal Proliferation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Wild-Type + Teriflunomide (50 &mu;M)</td><td>18.2 &plusmn; 1.4</td><td>< 0.2</td><td>85 &plusmn; 7</td><td>18.4 &plusmn; 1.5%</td><td>S-Phase Cell Cycle Arrest</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Wild-Type + Teriflunomide + Uridine</td><td>10.4 &plusmn; 0.8</td><td>< 0.2</td><td>420 &plusmn; 25</td><td>96.5 &plusmn; 4.8%</td><td>Rescued Proliferation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>UMP Synthase Deficient, Untreated</td><td>28.5 &plusmn; 2.1</td><td>84.5 &plusmn; 6.2</td><td>42 &plusmn; 4</td><td>12.1 &plusmn; 1.0%</td><td>Severe Megaloblastosis</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>UMP Synthase Deficient + Uridine (100 &mu;M)</td><td>11.2 &plusmn; 0.9</td><td>4.8 &plusmn; 0.4</td><td>435 &plusmn; 27</td><td>94.2 &plusmn; 4.5%</td><td>Phenotypic Rescue</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>UMP Synthase Deficient + Cytidine (100 &mu;M)</td><td>27.2 &plusmn; 1.9</td><td>81.2 &plusmn; 5.8</td><td>55 &plusmn; 5</td><td>24.5 &plusmn; 2.1%</td><td>Persistent Megaloblastosis</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
