        "e": "<strong>Differentiation of CPS-I and CPS-II (Topic 579):</strong> Mammalian cells possess two distinct carbamoyl phosphate synthetase isoenzymes with separate subcellular localizations and metabolic functions: (1) Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS-I) is localized exclusively in the **mitochondrial matrix** of hepatocytes. It utilizes free **ammonium ion** ($\\text{NH}_4^+$) as its nitrogen donor, requires $N$-acetylglutamate (NAG) as an obligate allosteric activator, and catalyzes the first committed step of the **urea cycle**. (2) Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II (CPS-II) is localized in the **cytosol** as part of the CAD multienzyme polypeptide. It utilizes the amide nitrogen of **L-glutamine** as its nitrogen donor, does not require NAG, is feedback-inhibited by UTP, and catalyzes the rate-limiting committed step of **de novo pyrimidine biosynthesis**.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A exhibits complete compartmental and nitrogen source inversion between the two isoenzymes.<br>&bull; C is incorrect because CPS-II is cytosolic (not mitochondrial), and UTP inhibits CPS-II rather than CPS-I.<br>&bull; D incorrectly places both enzymes in the cytosol and confuses the immediate enzymatic product (both produce carbamoyl phosphate, not orotate or citrulline directly)."
